 them the  empty circles the hollow circles  actually represent the projected points  and then we can  essentially replace this cluster of  points with a single line  that  is kind of a pattern recognition we have  identified the entire pattern of zebras  let us say with with that line so we can  say if my point lies near about that my  query point lies near that it's a zebra  what does this really buy for us  well  in some sense this is a little bit like  big picture formation in our heads when  a soccer coach is looking the soccer  coach is looking from outside the  field and the soccer coach is seeing the  whole game  the coach is not seeing very details too  much details but getting a big picture  of the game  a player with the ball is seeing a lot  more detail but is lacking the big  picture consequently it's easier for the  soccer coach to actually analyze the  game better a very similar thing is  happening here we are getting rid of  these small variations which you can  think of as details and we are getting  the big picture that most of the  distribution is along this line  as a result  this simplification  might help us to get a better do a  better analysis of the  points  and of course we have created a more  compact representation which takes less  space that itself is a win  so as a result  this trick which is known as  dimensionality reduction dimensional  reduction because this was a two  dimensional distribution of points for  instance we have reduced it to a single  line which is to say a one dimensional  entity that is why it's called  dimensionality reduction this  facilitates big picture analysis and  also it creates a simpler representation  simpler and more compact and less  expensive representation  so that  in some sense is the core idea behind  principle component analysis we will get  into more details  for the very soon but i just wanted to  leave you with a you know synopsis of  what we are going to do in this session  right at the beginning let's now  try to look at a real life problem  although we will look at a toy version  of a real life problem but it is a real  life problem nonetheless that real life  problem is document retrieval let's say  we have a set of documents in this  little example we have only seven  documents d0 through d6 and each  document is very small a few words  however this is still a valid example  and  the  problem is we will get a new document  and we will have to retrieve  documents from this from our library of  documents  in order of  similarity that is the most similar  document should be ranked first the next  closest document should be ranked second  and so forth  you can easily imagine where this is  very important google for instance would  need to solve this problem constantly  meaning this is their bread and butter  the technique i'm going to discuss right  now is not the only thing google does  it's a simplified version but still this  problem is extremely important for  google what do we do at this stage you  might have realized that the problem  essentially boils down to estimating  similarity of a pair of documents if we  solve that we have solved everything in  this document retrieval so given a pair  of documents we have to come up with a  quantitative way of  scoring their similarity the very first  thing to do is to have a quantitative  representation of the documents itself  this we do with some with a trick called  term frequency  essentially we imagine a very long  vector  this vector's length is same as our  vocabulary's length that is every word  in our vocabulary  has a specific slot  in this vector so if we go by uh  dictionary ordering the first slot may  correspond to aardvark or something and  the last slot will probably correspond  to a word like zymos starting with a z  in the middle we will have all other  words in our dictionary so what we do is  we go into the document for let's say  how many times artwork  appears in this document  zero so we will enter a zero next to  artwork  how many times zymos appears in this  document zero  however we will enter two  next two roses because roses rose  appears twice in this document and so we  will create a vector corresponding to  every document  so  this table  shows  such a factor from those six seven  documents that these you will notice  that roses we have a do and just to  simplify we are kind of pretending that  our vocabulary has only our dictionary  has only four words  i told you this is a toy example then  gun appears one once in document one and  violence appears one you will see a  score of one against each of this and  the word america also appears once and  hence  we have a score of one against america  and so forth so each row is a document  term frequency vector  this is as good as any other way this is  a pretty good way to actually create a  document descriptor vector so to say  one thing to be noted here is we are not  paying attention to  ordering or sequence of words this is a  so-called bag of words model we are just  if a word appears anywhere it cannot  distinguish between words appearing in  one sequence versus another it just  counts the number of occurrences of each  word anyway that's not a bad way may not  be the most sophisticated but not a bad  way to describe a document this bag also  we will work with this term frequency  back of words model now  how  uh  the  geometric view  corresponding to this is shown here  what you will notice is  each word is corresponding to a separate  axis and the number of the coordinate  corresponding to that axis is the number  of occurrences of the word  and this way  we will get a value for each coordinate  and by combining all of them each of  these points  is corresponding to a single document  there is one specific property  deliberately created so  is that  in most of the documents the word gun  and violence appears the same number of  times here their gun once violence once  here gone twice 